From a03cbc1f6730642da3e7932dfe2b3215d9baa991 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ethan Lee <ethantlee21@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:49:42 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: make idle render targets volatile on macOS

A visible surface keeps three full-size render targets. On a 6K display
one full-screen surface is 5588x3186 px, 68 MB for each target, and the
three stay in memory while the surface is idle.

When a target drew no frame for 1 s, the GPU completed its frame, it is
not the target of the newest frame and the layer does not show it, the
renderer makes its IOSurface volatile. Volatile memory does not count in
the footprint of the process, and the system can take it when it needs
memory. Before a frame draws to the target, the renderer makes it
nonvolatile again. Each frame clears its target before it draws, so a
target whose memory the system took loses nothing.

The render thread marks the targets after each draw and with a timer at
the time the next target can become idle. With a blinking cursor, which
draws each 600 ms, one or two of the three targets stay volatile.

The swap chain keeps three targets, because two targets make the CPU
wait for the GPU at high refresh rates.
